From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:43:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nak: NAK_UNIFORM_HOIST, uniform values out of divergent
 control flow

NAK gives a value a UGPR only if both the value and its block are
uniform: in divergent control flow every value goes to a GPR (a warp runs
both sides of a branch, so a UGPR reused on one side could clobber a
value the other side still needs).  In the corpus 5.8% of the
instructions are uniform values in divergent blocks.

NAK_UNIFORM_HOIST (0-2, default 0) moves them to the nearest dominating
block in uniform control flow, where they get UGPRs: 1, ALU instructions
and constant buffer loads whose operands are available there (constants
are copied along; no global memory, no subgroup operations, since they
then run when no lane takes the branch); 2, only the constant buffer
loads and the ALU instructions that compute nothing but their addresses.

Hoisting makes live ranges longer and can cross an occupancy cliff, so a
shader the hoist changed is also compiled without it (from a copy of the
NIR), and the result with more warps per SM, or as many and fewer static
cycles, is kept.
